      "text": "To determine the digit \\( n \\) such that the number \\( 854n526 \\) is divisible by 11, we use the rule for divisibility by 11. According to this rule, a number is divisible by 11 if the difference between the sum of the digits in the odd positions and the sum of the digits in the even positions is a multiple of 11 (including zero).\n\nFirst, let's list the positions of the digits and their contributions to the sum based on their positions:\n- Positions 1, 3, 5, and 7 have even sums due to the positions: \\( 0+n+0+2 \\)\n- Positions 2, 4, and 6 have even sums due to the positions: \\( 8+5+6 \\)\n\nCalculate the sums for each:\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{even positions}} = 8 + 5 + 6 = 19\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{odd positions}} = n + 0 + 0 + 2 = n + 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the difference:\n\\[\n\\text{Difference} = (19) - (n + 2) = 19 - n - 2 = 17 - n\n\\]\n\nFor the number to be divisible by 11, \\( 17 - n \\) must be a multiple of 11. Let's set up the equation:\n\\[\n17 - n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[\n17 - n = 11k \\quad (\\text{for some integer } k)\n\\]\n\\[\n17 - n = 11k\n\\]\n\nWe need \\( 17 - n \\) to be a negative multiple of 11:\n\\[\n17 - n < 0 \\implies n > 17\n\\]\n\nSince \\( n \\) must be between 0 and 9 (inclusive) for digits in a valid number, there is no \\( n \\) in this range that makes \\( 17 - n \\) a multiple of 11.\n\nThus, the input \\( n \\) which qualified is concluded wrong and cannot be a correct answer.",
