      "text": "To determine the value of \\( n \\) that makes the seven-digit number \\( 854n526 \\) divisible by 11, we use the divisibility rule for 11. This rule states that a number is divisible by 11 if the difference between the sum of the digits in the odd positions and the sum of the digits in the even positions is a multiple of 11 (including 0).\n\nThe digits of the number \\( 854n526 \\) are as follows:\n- Odd positions: 8, 4, 5, 6\n- Even positions: 5, n, 2\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the digits in the odd positions:\n\\[ 8 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 23 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the sum of the digits in the even positions:\n\\[ 5 + n + 2 = 7 + n \\]\n\nAccording to the divisibility rule, the difference between these two sums must be a multiple of 11:\n\\[ (7 + n) - 23 = n - 16 \\]\n\nWe need \\( n - 16 \\) to be a multiple of 11. Let's list the multiples of 11:\n\\[ 11, -11, 22, -22, \\ldots \\]\n\nWe will test each of these values to see which one satisfies the equation \\( n - 16 = 0 \\) (the simplest case):\n1. \\( n - 16 = 11 \\implies n = 27 \\) (not possible since \\( n \\) must be a digit from 0 to 9)\n2. \\( n - 16 = -11 \\implies n = 5 \\) (possible since 5 is a digit from 0 to 9)\n3. \\( n - 16 = 22 \\implies n = 38 \\) (not possible since \\( n \\) must be a digit from 0 to 9)\n4. \\( n - 16 = -22 \\implies n = -15 \\) (not possible since \\( n \\) must be a digit from 0 to 9)\n\nThe only valid value for \\( n \\) that satisfies the condition is \\( n = 5 \\).\n\nThus, the value of \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
